Title: Enhancing CPU Scheduling and Fairness Using Ticket Lock in xv6
In this project, we are building an improved version of the xv6 operating system by adding two new features, a lottery based CPU scheduler and a fair ticket based locking system. This upgrades make the system fairer, more predictable. The idea is to control how processes get CPU time and how they access shared resources, ensuring fairness.
 
Feature 1: Adaptive Lottery Scheduler for Fair CPU Sharing
In this project, we upgrade the xv6 CPU scheduler into a workflow based adaptive lottery scheduler. Instead of using fixed tickets, processes can now update, request, exchange, and accumulate tickets during execution. This makes CPU allocation more dynamic, fair, and behavior based.
System Calls:
set_base_tickets(int n): Set or update the base ticket count of the current process.
update_ticket_status(struct pstat*): Returns full process ticket information, including:
1.     base tickets
2.     accumulated tickets
3.     exchanged tickets
4.     dependency info
5.     total CPU ticks
 
Short Implementation Overview
Workflow:
Process Init → Routine Execution → Ticket Update → Ticket Request → Ticket Exchange → Ticket Accumulation
 
Process Initialization
Each process starts with base tickets, zero accumulated tickets, and optional dependency ID.
 Routine Execution
When a process completes routine tasks, it earns reward tickets.
Ticket Update
Before each scheduling cycle, reward tickets are merged into the main ticket count.
Ticket Request
A process can ask for more tickets using:
set_base_tickets(current + extra)
This increases its chance to win the CPU lottery.
Ticket Exchange
Dependent processes (parent–child or cooperative tasks) can share or donate tickets to each other.
Ticket Accumulation
Unused reward tickets accumulate over time, giving waiting processes a fairer chance to run.
 
Updated Lottery Scheduling Logic
The scheduler uses:
effective_tickets = base_tickets + accumulated_tickets - exchanged_tickets
The process with the winning ticket gets the CPU.
 

 
Feature 2: Ticket Lock for Fairness
We are going to add a simple, fair ticket based spin lock in xv6 operating system. Every process gets a turn number when requesting the lock, and only the process with the current turn can enter. This prevents unfairness or starvation caused by the default xv6 spinlock.
 
Short Implementation Method:
 Adding a ticket number and a turn number to the lock. When a process tries to lock, it gets a ticket and waits until it’s ticket matches the turn. When it finishes, it increases the turn so the next process can enter.
 
Evaluation
 We will measure fairness in two ways:
1.CPU Fairness Graph:
  Three processes with a ticket ratio 3:2:1 will run, and a gantt chart will show how many time slices each process gets. The counts should follow the same ratio over time.
2.Lock Fairness Test:
  Multiple processes will repeatedly acquire and release the new ticket lock, and results should show that every process gets the lock in exact arrival order, with no starvation.
